      ],
      "body_md": "For **brushed drive**, choose the controller around the motor's measured or manufacturer-rated stall current, voltage, and whether you want active current limiting. Repeat's Budget Ant DESC is a simple 2A dual controller with a BEC and optional mixing, but it explicitly has **no active current limiting**. The Dominion adds per-channel current limiting and thermal protection at a higher weight. Turnabot's Malenki boards combine three brushed channels with an AFHDS2A receiver, which can remove a separate receiver and a lot of wiring.\n\nFor **brushless drive**, AM32 is common because it supports true bidirectional operation and tunable startup/current behavior. Repeat's Micro, Single, and Dual boards trade size, channel count, voltage range, and current-control features. The Catalyst goes further by putting dual AM32 drive channels and an AFHDS2A receiver on one 4 g board.\n\nDo not select an ESC only by the large amp number in its product name. Firmware, current limiting, cooling, battery voltage, motor winding, wiring, and repeated stalls matter. For small robots, a controller with a lower configured current limit can be safer and more driveable than a physically larger ESC run unrestricted."
